     "body": "<p>After some more thought, most of what would go into a level 1 compliant implementation would probably fit best into a Fedora-Create project of objects and Pluggable modules:</p>\n\n<p>1. A package of default content models (for node and resource types) and system objects (root node)<br/>\n2. A DOManager/Reader implementation that would assign UUIDs and inject a default location for node objects that don't specify a parent node<br/>\n3. A FieldSearch module to index the parent-child relationships and node types<br/>\n4. A BasicServer extension or a new module that implements the javax.jcr.Repository interface and listens on an RMI interface, including a way to authenticate clients in a manageable way (ideally through the user filter? or maybe as back-end security?)</p>\n\n<p>The one immediately obvious departure is that the JSR specifies node type inheritance, which doesn't exist yet in the Fedora CMA ( see <a href=\"https://fedora-commons.org/confluence/spaces/viewthread.action?key=DEV&amp;id=13140061\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://fedora-commons.org/confluence/spaces/viewthread.action?key=DEV&amp;id=13140061</a> for thoughts, including implementation efforts under the ECM project).  This would also bring to the fore the question of service deployment resolution for Fedora objects.</p>\n\n<p>As an aside, the JSR 170 DOM-like model of nodes and properties is structurally similar to the aggregator/resource model of NCore ( <a href=\"http://ncore.nsdl.org/index.php?menu=services&amp;submenu=services!EduPak\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://ncore.nsdl.org/index.php?menu=services&amp;submenu=services!EduPak</a> ) and not unlike the RODA data model ( <a href=\"http://www.fedora-commons.org/about/examples/roda\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://www.fedora-commons.org/about/examples/roda</a> )</p>",
     "created": "02/Feb/10 12:50 PM",
